{"reference":"Isaiah 7","book":"Isaiah","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Immanuel Sign","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Isaiah 7 places the Immanuel sign within Ahaz's immediate political crisis, alongside Isaiah's challenge to Judah's reliance on foreign coalitions and military resources. The sign is therefore connected to the chapter's urgent question of trust amid threat. The child's immediate identity, and how this setting relates to Matthew's later use of the passage, remain disputed.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 7:1-17","Isaiah 7:1-25","Isaiah 7:1-9","Isaiah 7:14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_007_8db5a709358fd5f33b71b03a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Matthew narrates Jesus' conception from the Holy Spirit and reads Isaiah 7:14 through the Greek wording, including its term parthenos, together with the name Emmanuel. This is a later interpretation of Isaiah's verse; it should be distinguished from the question of what the sign meant within Isaiah 7 itself.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 7:14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_007_146ca67454265eed78e5d0b2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Isaiah as a Received Book","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The Great Isaiah Scroll is an almost complete witness to all sixty-six chapters of Isaiah. It broadly aligns with the Masoretic tradition while also containing many spelling and substantive differences; other Qumran Isaiah manuscripts and interpretations likewise show that the book was actively transmitted and interpreted.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_007_13141b4f37fbe76a6b9508f3"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}